<!--
  What the assistant needs on Android, declared here so the host app does not
  have to. The manifest merger adds both to the app's own manifest at build
  time.

  INTERNET: Flutter's app template grants it only to debug and profile
  builds, so a release build of an app that never needed the network before
  would ship an assistant that cannot reach its API.
  RECORD_AUDIO: the composer's built-in microphone. The runtime prompt is
  still the system's; this only makes asking possible.

  The queries are not permissions. Since Android 11 an app sees only the
  apps it names, and opening a card's link in an in-app browser needs to see
  one that serves https and Custom Tabs. The composer's Camera needs to see
  the camera app: image_picker grants it the file to write the photograph
  into by looking it up, and a camera it cannot see is handed nothing.
  Reading answers aloud needs to see the phone's speech engine, or
  flutter_tts finds none and says nothing.

  No CAMERA permission, on purpose. The camera app takes the picture under
  its own; declaring it here would make the Play Store hide every host app
  from devices without a camera. An app that declares it itself still gets
  the runtime prompt, which image_picker asks for.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECORD_AUDIO" />
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
            <data android:scheme="https" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.support.customtabs.action.CustomTabsService" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.media.action.IMAGE_CAPTURE" />
        </intent>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.TTS_SERVICE" />
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
